# Anti_GEO project rules

## Repository

- **This repo only**: `JunyiL123/Anti_GEO` — https://github.com/JunyiL123/Anti_GEO
- **Never push to** `GEO-optim/GEO` (upstream Princeton code; read-only for us)
- Project root: `Anti-GEO/` with `src/anti_geo/`, `demo/`, `tests/`, `geo-optimizer/`

## Git workflow (local + cloud agents)

**Start of session (mandatory, no user prompt needed):**
- Run this **before answering the first message** in any Anti-GEO session
- Do not wait for the user to ask; `git fetch` alone is not enough
- Uses plain `git` only — `gh` is not required

```bash
# Works from repo root or any subdirectory — do not `cd Anti-GEO` (fails if workspace is already Anti-GEO)
git -C "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)" pull origin main
```

**End of session (after user approves or explicitly asks to commit):**
```bash
git add <relevant files>
git commit -m "concise message"
git push origin main
```

- Prefer feature branches (`feature/...`) for large changes; merge via PR when possible
- Never commit `.env`, `.venv/`, API keys, or credentials
- Only commit when the user asks (unless pre-commit hook auto-fix requires amend)

## Code layout

| Path | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `src/anti_geo/` | Production pipeline (fetch → score → decide) |
| `demo/` | Simulations and CLIs (`defense_demo.py`, `analyze_url.py`) |
| `geo-optimizer/` | Vendored Princeton GEO (attack/benchmark); cite upstream |
| `tests/` | `pytest` offline tests |

Run tests: `PYTHONPATH=src python3 -m pytest tests/ -q`

## Defense design principles

- No blanket blocking of UGC domains (Reddit, Wikipedia, etc.)
- No perplexity-only filters for GEO detection
- Three layers: semantic scoring (L1), source trust (L2), synthesis guard (L3)
- Downrank/gate rather than hard-block when uncertain
- Distinguish **mention** vs **endorsement** in synthesis

## Python conventions

- Python 3.11+, match existing style in `src/anti_geo/`
- Minimal diffs; no over-engineering
- Infer trust/scores from signals — avoid hardcoded per-domain labels in production code
